Task: Transform, pixel snapping and the sub-pixel accumulator (Phase 2)

Red phase: tests first, failed on the missing module. Green phase: eleven new
tests pass, thirty-seven in total.

What the tests pin down:
- Moving, stretching and turning behave as expected, including a quarter turn
  mapping one axis onto the other.
- Combining two transforms produces the same result as applying them one after
  the other, with the order stated explicitly, since getting that backwards is
  the classic mistake and silently produces plausible-looking wrong motion.
- Snapping produces whole numbers.
- The accumulator emits whole numbers only.
- Over three hundred steps of a tenth of a pixel, the total lands within half a
  pixel of the truth. Rounding each step independently would have produced zero
  movement across that entire sequence, which is the exact failure this exists
  to prevent.
- Negative movement behaves the same way.
- The carried remainder never grows beyond half a pixel.

Design note:
A transform is stored as the six meaningful numbers of a matrix rather than a
nested structure, because every later layer will build these per frame and the
cost of allocating them adds up.
